          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does guidance describe about exercise and anxiety?",
          "answer": "The sources I could retrieve do not include a clinical guideline that makes a recommendation about exercise for anxiety disorders. I also could not find a systematic review or meta-analysis, so I can't say how strong the evidence is or whether authorities disagree.\n\n**What the sources say**\n\n- The generalized anxiety disorder summary lists structured exercise, relaxation, mindfulness and guided digital programs as self-management approaches with evidence. It does not give strength ratings. (*Generalized anxiety disorder*, condition summary)\n- A general lifestyle note says moderate aerobic activity across the week has \"the most supporting evidence,\" with resistance training having \"a smaller but real\" body of support. It describes activity as an adjunct to first-line treatment, not a replacement. This is an educational summary, not a graded guideline, and it cites no study numbers. (*Physical activity and anxiety*, lifestyle note)\n- The same note says exercise can raise heart rate and breathing in ways that feel like anxiety, which sometimes leads people to avoid it. It also says a short routine that is kept up tends to beat an ambitious one that is dropped.\n\n**Strength of evidence and disagreement**\n\nThe sources describe aerobic exercise as the best-supported type, but they do not grade the evidence or give effect sizes, so I can't rate its strength. The only disagreement the search returned concerned knee and hip osteoarthritis injections, which is unrelated to anxiety. I found no documented disagreement between authorities about exercise for anxiety.\n\n**Safety**\n\nIf exercise brings on chest pain, fainting, or breathlessness out of proportion to the effort, stop and get medical assessment promptly. A first panic attack with chest pain or breathlessness also needs urgent assessment, because a heart or lung cause cannot be ruled out by how it feels.\n\nIf you are considering exercise for anxiety, a doctor or other clinician can help you judge whether it fits your situation and what kind of activity to start with.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
